<https://www.ebi.ac.uk/biostudies/bioimages/studies/S-BIAD1488#5636acd9-6201-4cad-8d21-b4ce782d1eac> a schema1:LabProtocol ;
    schema1:description """We built a ∆BLX equipped with two long working distance (WD) objectives: a customized excitation objective (numerical aperture (N.A.) = 0.5, WD = 11.7 mm) to provide good optical sectioning capability, and an Olympus detection objective (N.A. = 0.6, WD = 8 mm) to accommodate the thickness of the freestanding expanded gel. Direct sample scanning was performed using a voice coil stage (V308, Physik Instrumente) featuring a long travel distance of Z=7 mm, which was run in closed loop and analog input mode, allowing us to tile scan the freestanding hydrogel that was glued onto an L-shaped sample holder. The unit volume comprised ~1400 x 2048 pixels x Z steps at a pixel size of 0.325 µm, corresponding to 457 µm x 665 µm x Z mm (x,y,z), where Z ≤ 7 depending on gel thickness, with 20% overlapped region applied for stitching.

Image stitching from file chunks was performed with the ImarisStitcher software or Fiji plugin Big Stitcher, running on our Windows workstations equipped with dual Intel Xeon Gold 6256 CPUs (total 24 CPU cores, 1 TB memory, and 90 TB storage for working space). All imaging rendering and figure preparations were conducted in Amira software (ThermoFisher Scientific) and ITK-SNAP open-source software.""" ;
    schema1:labEquipment "Axicon-based Bessel lightsheet microscope" ;
    schema1:measurementTechnique obo:FBbi_00000241,
        obo:FBbi_00000251,
        obo:FBbi_00000345,
        obo:FBbi_00000369 ;
