<https://www.ebi.ac.uk/biostudies/bioimages/studies/S-BIAD1507#195aaced-f7f9-411c-b718-c7fe245e297b> a schema1:LabProtocol ;
    schema1:description """MIBI run setup:
Quantitative MIBI-TOF imaging was performed using a commercial MIBIScope instrument (IonPath, Menlo Park, USA). For each tissue microarray (TMA) core on each TMA, we used paired H&E images to identify the subregion that would be acquired on MIBI, avoiding areas of necrosis or empty slide. TMA cores were named by their row and column number in the TMA grid to enable easy mapping back to the appropriate metadata. To ensure that the cores were reliably assigned the correct row and column coordinates on the TMA, we added automated checks to identify naming errors and fix them following user confirmation. Prior to beginning data acquisition, the order of the cores was randomized to mitigate potential batch effects from instrument drift. 
  
Acquisition settings:
We used the same settings across all MIBI data acquired in this study. The size of each field of view (FOV) was determined based on sample availability. When possible, FOVs of size (800 um)^2 and (2048 pixels^)2 were acquired. However, if insufficient tissue was present, an FOV size of (400 um)^2 and (1024 pixels)^2 was used instead. We used a custom preset of 8.0v beam current and 0.63ms dwell time to balance acquisition speed and image clarity. We disabled all default background correction and noise removal settings. 

MIBI-TOF cohort characteristics
We used MIBI-TOF imaging to generate high quality, multiplexed imaging data with subcellular resolution across 17 TMAs. The total dataset consists of 548 fields of view from 314 patients in the Spain TNBC study and 277 fields of view from 142 patients in the Stanford TNBC study. """ ;
    schema1:labEquipment "MIBIScope instrument" ;
    schema1:name "MIBI acquisition" .

